# Slater exponents for the extended Huckel engine, by element.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR.  A correlation diagram's two fragment columns come
# from geometry and group theory alone, but its middle column -- the
# molecular orbitals, to scale -- needs energies from somewhere.  For
# teaching, where the ORDERING is the whole content and the third
# significant figure is not, extended Huckel supplies them from
# nothing but the structure: no calculation to run, no code to
# install, no output to parse.
#
# It is NOT a substitute for a calculation and must not be used when
# one exists.  It gets orderings right and energies roughly, which is
# exactly the right limitation for a qualitative diagram and exactly
# the wrong one for anything quantitative.
#
# The diagonal elements H_ii come from ValenceOrbitalEnergies, which
# already holds them: they are the same valence orbital potential
# energies a textbook correlation diagram is drawn from.  This file
# adds the other half, the radial extent of each orbital, as Slater
# exponents in inverse bohr.
#
# PROVENANCE.  Single-zeta Slater exponents of the usual kind -- the
# values Hoffmann's extended Huckel work and the textbooks that follow
# it use, from Slater's rules with the standard adjustments for
# hydrogen and the first row.  Sources differ in the third figure and
# it does not matter here; what matters is that one element's orbitals
# are larger or smaller than another's in the right order.
#
# symbol  n   zeta(s)  zeta(p)
